{
  "id": "ep030",
  "duration": 50.716,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.075,
      "text": "A single teaspoon of a neutron star weighs one billion tons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s concept of magnetar 1E 1547.0-5408.png",
        "credit": "NASA/Pablo Garcia",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_concept_of_magnetar_1E_1547.0-5408.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.367,
          "end": 4.067,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.075,
      "end": 11.47,
      "text": "Neutron stars are the gravitationally collapsed cores of massive stars formed following a supernova explosion.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Chandra 3-color X-ray Image of Cassiopeia A (2004-casa-more-1).tiff",
        "credit": "(Credit: NASA/CXC/GSFC/U.Hwang et al.)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandra_3-color_X-ray_Image_of_Cassiopeia_A_(2004-casa-more-1).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.067,
          "end": 6.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.9,
          "end": 11.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.47,
      "end": 20.44,
      "text": "In nineteen sixty-seven, graduate student Jocelyn Bell Burnell detected a radio pulse so rhythmic she nicknamed it Little Green Men.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.467,
          "end": 20.433,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.44,
      "end": 26.035,
      "text": "These stellar remnants pack the mass of a sun into a sphere only twelve miles wide.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Chandra 3-color X-ray Image of Cassiopeia A (2004-casa-more-1).tiff",
        "credit": "(Credit: NASA/CXC/GSFC/U.Hwang et al.)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandra_3-color_X-ray_Image_of_Cassiopeia_A_(2004-casa-more-1).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.433,
          "end": 26.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.035,
      "end": 34.155,
      "text": "The extreme objects rotate hundreds of times per second and generate magnetic fields trillions of times stronger than Earth's.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The polarisation of light emitted by a neutron star (eso1641a).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_polarisation_of_light_emitted_by_a_neutron_star_(eso1641a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.033,
          "end": 28.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 28.9,
          "end": 34.167,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 34.155,
      "end": 40.405,
      "text": "In twenty seventeen, scientists finally heard two of them collide through gravitational waves.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Eso1733q Artist\u2019s impression of merging neutron stars.jpg",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada/M. Kornmesser",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eso1733q_Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_merging_neutron_stars.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 34.167,
          "end": 38.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 38.3,
          "end": 40.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 40.405,
      "end": 50.366,
      "text": "These cosmic impacts created the heavy gold and platinum found in our jewelry today, leaving behind a single teaspoon of material that weighs one billion tons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s concept of magnetar 1E 1547.0-5408.png",
        "credit": "NASA/Pablo Garcia",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_concept_of_magnetar_1E_1547.0-5408.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 40.4,
          "end": 50.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
